Title: "Type System"
Id: "6797835a-79b0-45ab-8ac7-1db592e2b290"

Text: "The checker tracks type facts such as `G is \set` and specification facts
such as `x \"in\" G`. Facts come from headings, `using:`, `when:`, theorem
`given:`, local clause bindings, `where:`, structural declarations, and assumed
clauses.

A `Declares:` item defines a reusable nominal type or interface. Its `extends:`
rules are recursive: knowing `G is \group` may establish `G is \monoid` and
then `G is \magma`. A `Defines:` item creates a typed value; `Realizes:` creates
a concrete implementation of an abstract definition. Capabilities owned by a
`Declares:` type apply to every instance, while capabilities owned by
`Defines:` or `Realizes:` apply to that defined command value with its parameters
substituted.

Builtin types use two leading backslashes:

- `\\type` holds for command references whose top-level item is `Declares:`
- `\\statement`, `\\expression`, and `\\specification` classify formulation
  values
- `\\anything` is satisfied by every declared value but adds no concrete type
  fact
- `\\abstract` is the abstract capability target

Literal command arguments may satisfy nominal requirements structurally:
functions by input/output shape, tuples by positional component types, and
collections by the declared element pattern and specs. Named values still need
ordinary facts.

Bindings are processed in order. `::=` introduces a structural subject and its
expansion; a binding-position `:=` introduces its left target, while symbols
used only on the right must already be known. A defined name may not be
introduced twice or shadowed in the inherited scope.

Equality and inequality are accepted for declared symbols even without a
type-specific equality capability."
Id: "ef25a78c-c06d-43c1-8bec-b53f99f00e2f"
